## 1. The lattice — non-obvious connections this thesis activates

- **Design & Construction ↔ HOK international-concourse team ↔ FAA ATP
  scoring.** The 1.735-million-sq-ft international expansion at IAD is being
  designed by HOK right now, and its follow-on Federal Inspection Services
  facility is roughly 176,000 sq ft [1]. The design manual is the leverage
  point: an acoustic/PA standard inserted before that program reaches ~30%
  design becomes program cost. Inserted after, it is a change order. That
  single insertion also converts "accessibility" from a value statement into
  a scoring credit under the FAA Airport Terminal Program, which lists
  "expand access for persons with disabilities" as a stated selection
  criterion [2]. One document; two payoffs.

- **BWI ↔ regional traveler ↔ MWAA's Maryland-appointed board members.** BWI
  Thurgood Marshall joined the Hidden Disabilities Sunflower Program in May
  2025 [3]. In a three-airport regional market where the same traveler
  chooses among BWI, DCA, and IAD, the quiet-by-design standard has an
  obvious diplomatic partner. Adding Sunflower at MWAA now, and framing it
  as *regional coherence with a Maryland peer*, is a low-cost win that plays
  directly to the three Maryland-appointed board seats without asking them
  to spend political capital.

- **Emergency Management ↔ Public Safety ↔ NFPA 72 intelligibility floor.**
  NFPA 72 requires a measured Speech Transmission Index of at least 0.45 in
  90 percent of measurement locations per acoustically distinguishable
  space, with an average of at least 0.50 [4]. That is the *floor*, and
  most airport hold-rooms, gate lounges, and baggage halls do not verify
  compliance building-wide. Quiet-by-design done right maps every ADS,
  measures STI at commissioning, and gives the Emergency Manager and the
  Director of Public Safety a defensible evidence base after the January
  2025 midair collision made post-incident communication a live board-level
  question.

- **Concessions and clubs ↔ dwell and spend ↔ United Club at Concourse E.**
  Concourse E opens in September 2026 with 46,000 sq ft of concessions and
  a 40,000-sq-ft United Club [5][6]. Concessionaires and premium-club
  operators are natural allies of a quiet standard because a legible sonic
  environment lengthens dwell and lifts non-aeronautical revenue. This is
  the constituency the standard needs, and nobody has asked them.

- **Airline Use & Lease MII ↔ program cost vs. scope ↔ 2030 CPE trajectory.**
  Airline CPE is forecast to move from $12.77 to $20.71 by 2030 under the
  CCP [7]. Any standard added mid-project as new scope becomes an MII
  argument. A standard added at the design-manual level, applied at project
  inception, is materially harder to classify as MII-triggering because it
  is baseline program cost rather than incremental scope. The vehicle
  matters more than the content.

- **Historic preservation (Saarinen Main Terminal) ↔ acoustics ↔ adaptive
  reuse.** The Saarinen terminal is a designated icon and one of the most
  reverberant public halls in North American aviation. A quiet-by-design
  standard opens the door to acoustic remediation the historic-preservation
  community can actually endorse: soft goods, floor systems, absorptive
  furnishings, distributed low-density loudspeakers. Without a standard,
  every intervention is treated as an aesthetic infringement.

- **DCA slot economics ↔ Section 502 long-hauls ↔ missed-boarding cost.**
  Section 502 of the 2024 FAA Reauthorization added ten daily beyond-
  perimeter exemptions at DCA; a missed boarding on a slot-exempt SEA, SFO,
  LAS, SAT, or SAN flight is not a seat problem, it is a slot problem. The
  quiet-terminal standard's most important operational metric at DCA is
  therefore *missed-boarding rate*, not *decibels*. Nobody outside airline
  operations frames it this way, and that is the frame that will unlock
  their support.

- **White House ↔ Dulles narrative ↔ political currency at zero federal
  cost.** The July 2026 Dulles announcement was joint MWAA / United /
  USDOT / White House. "Quiet, dignified, orderly" is a message the current
  administration can carry, and it costs no federal dollars. That is a rare
  configuration; it should be used.

