## 2. Three cross-disciplinary pattern matches

### Pattern 1 — HCAHPS "Quiet at Night" and CMS Value-Based Purchasing (2007–present)

Before 2007, U.S. hospitals treated overnight noise as an operational nuisance
the way most airports today treat terminal soundscape. That changed when the
HCAHPS survey embedded "Quiet at Night" as a scored patient-experience
measure and CMS tied roughly 25% of the Value-Based Purchasing Total
Performance Score to HCAHPS results [8]. The behavioral response was
striking: hospitals reorganized overnight nursing rounds, adopted vibrating
pagers, replaced squeaky wheels on floor cleaners, and specified acoustic
finishes into renovations they would not have specified otherwise. The
finding: *what you measure is what you renovate.*

**Mapping to MWAA.** A quiet-by-design standard without a scoreboard is a
design manual entry. A quiet-by-design standard with a scored MWAA-internal
metric — STI at commissioning, dB(A) LEQ by zone, announcement frequency,
missed-boarding rate, exclusion-complaint rate — becomes an operating
discipline. HCAHPS is the proof that measurement changes behavior more
reliably than requirements do. MWAA can copy the mechanism directly.

### Pattern 2 — The Berkeley curb-cut, September 28, 1971

In 1971 the Berkeley "Rolling Quads," led by Ed Roberts, poured concrete and
took jackhammers to city curbs at night to force the city to build
wheelchair ramps [9]. The city relented and made curb cuts standard in
commercial districts. What happened next is the point: curb cuts, designed
for one small user group, turned out to be beneficial for parents with
strollers, delivery workers with hand trucks, cyclists, skateboarders,
and — the population that dominates airport terminals — travelers with
wheeled luggage.

**Mapping to MWAA.** Design the sonic environment for the traveler who is
hard-of-hearing, autistic, cognitively fatigued, non-English-speaking, or
smartphone-less, and the airport becomes measurably better for the
frequent business traveler who represents most of DCA's federal-adjacent
demand. The design-for-the-tail-defines-the-median principle is
disproportionately powerful in airports precisely because 5% of passengers
in "sensory distress" produce a far larger share of missed connections,
gate-agent interruptions, and negative-experience surveys than their share
of headcount predicts. The curb-cut lens also reframes accessibility from
"cost center" to "median-experience upgrade" — which is how it gets funded.

### Pattern 3 — SFO's Quiet Airport Program (2018–2020)

SFO implemented gate-only boarding calls in 2020, reports a 40% reduction in
boarding-area announcements, and claims roughly 90 minutes of noise removed
per day in the International Terminal [10]. It is the nearest domestic peer
this thesis has. It is also a cautionary tale: SFO drew visible complaints
from travelers who don't use airline apps and don't hear terminal-wide calls
in the way they used to.

**Mapping to MWAA.** The lesson is not "quieter is bad." The lesson is that a
quiet standard is only as strong as its *redundant channels*. Where SFO
under-invested was in signaling the alternatives — the app push, the visual
gate call, the seat-back FIDS in the concourse, the Sunflower lanyard, the
human at the podium. MWAA can arrive at the same operational outcome (40%
fewer announcements) *and* the accessibility outcome (measurable exclusion
rate falling) by writing the redundant-channel requirements *first* and the
announcement-reduction targets *second*. Sequence is strategy.

