## 4. Collaboration paths — the two hardest oppositions

### Opposition 1 — Signatory airlines (United at IAD, American at DCA)

*Their reasonable objection:* "This will cost us missed boardings, complicate
IROP calls, and might trigger MII."

*What they actually need:* United needs Concourse E and the follow-on
international expansion to feel premium. Their new 40,000-sq-ft club, their
concessions program, and their international transfer product all improve
in an intelligible sonic environment. American at DCA has the more
sensitive missed-boarding math (slot economics) and therefore the strongest
reason to *want* a boarding-communication standard that is measurably
better than the current PA-only default.

*Trade to offer:* Joint pilot at United's Concourse E gates with a shared
metric (missed-boarding rate). If it moves in United's favor, the
airline-facing case writes itself. Structure the standard as design-manual
baseline (not MII scope) and give the airlines a formal comment window,
not a veto.

*First convening:* MWAA D&C + Airline Affairs + United station leadership +
American station leadership at DCA, held before the incremental Dulles
scope's MII package is finalized. Agenda: one metric (missed-boarding
rate), one instrumented pilot (Concourse E), one commitment (the standard
is design-manual, not MII scope).

### Opposition 2 — Legacy PA vendors and integrators

*Their reasonable objection:* Performance-based specifications erode the
seller's incumbency advantage in loudspeaker density and channel count.

*What they actually need:* Predictable pipeline. A performance-based
standard is not a threat if the pipeline expands, and this pipeline
expands materially — Concourse E, the HOK international expansion, DCA
gate refresh, and the AeroTrain extension all become PA/audio programs
inside the same 24-month window.

*Trade to offer:* Convert the specification from "product density" to
"measured intelligibility at commissioning." Same vendors, larger pipeline,
different competitive metric. The good vendors win. The order-taking
vendors lose.

*First convening:* An industry day with a draft performance specification,
convened by MWAA D&C and Procurement, six months before the design-manual
change is issued. The reframing beats the surprise.

