1. "The quiet-airport question is not silence versus announcements. It is
   intelligibility versus noise. Only one of those has an ANSI-recognized
   metric, and MWAA does not currently require it building-wide."

2. "The window closes when the HOK international concourse crosses 30%
   design. Before that, this is a design guideline. After, it is a change
   order."

3. "SFO cut boarding announcements by 40% and saved roughly 90 minutes of
   daily noise. The complaints it drew came almost entirely from travelers
   the redundant channels forgot. That is a design failure, not a policy
   failure — and it is the one MWAA has the opportunity to avoid."

4. "The airline economics inverted the moment slot-exempt long-hauls started
   boarding at DCA. A missed boarding at DCA is not a seat problem
   anymore; it is a slot problem. That is the frame that will bring the
   airlines to the table."

5. "The curb cut was built for wheelchair users and turned out to be for
   everyone with wheeled luggage. The Sunflower lanyard, the visual gate
   call, the app push and the human at the podium are the twenty-first-
   century curb cut of the airport terminal. Design the standard for the
   traveler at the edge, and the median passenger gets the upgrade."

